m at p200 that continues on a  little bit through  and past p400  i'm showing you here a number of  different genotypes that have different  amounts of either atm or aptx  the purple being that of the wild type  and then the orange  being that of the double mutant an  expression of at least one copy of the  atm  um or the aptx  gene although i'm not showing that one  here  uh is sufficient to alleviate the  symptomology in these mice and this is  suggests that if one was able to restore  some of the atm protein  that would probably have a major effect  on these mice  we next wanted to look at the  what it was that was atrophying within  the cerebellum and we took a look at  the cortex of the cerebellum in  particular we measured the molecular  layer and granule cell layer  of the  cerebellar cortex the molecular layer  containing the purkinje neuron dendrites  which is a primary cell within  the cerebellar cortex and what we found  was that there in  we found in the wild type mouse uh no  change in the molecular layer width  across age however we did find a  significant  reduction in the  prekenji neuron in the parking neuron  dendritic layer that molecular layer  over time and we found these effects  across both the medial intermediate and  lateral areas of the cerebellum  we did not find any change in the  granule cell layer suggesting that there  is a relatively selective effect going  on within the purkinje neurons in  [Music]  the cerebellum and so we decided to take  a closer look to see whether there were  physiological disruptions that were also  going on within the purkinje neurons  to do this we examined uh the  electrophysiological the properties of  bricking neurons here we looked at their  neural  their neural signaling that is their act  the action potential that purkinje  neurons generate spontaneously  they're a little bit different than most  other neurons that they will fire these  action potentials at 40 to 120 hertz  without any excitation  we did this by taking acute slices of  the brain having them  incubated in an artificial cerebral  spinal fluid and then just took an all  an extra cellular electrode right up  next to the fricking neuron and recorded  the electrical impulses which you can  see in this purple and orange traces on  the right here  in the wild type on the top you can see  that there's a fast set of action  potentials each of those vertical lines  is an action potential and that the rate  of these action potentials is  significantly decreased in the double  mutant  we found that this was developmentally  regulated although we found  across the different regions the lateral  intermediate and medial  that the  firing rate was reduced as early as um  p45 postnatal day 45 and that it did  reduce um significantly down at  postnatal day 200  into p400  and that  expression of either atm or aptx was  sufficient to alleviate  this phenotype  to prevent this phenotype  we then took a closer look to see what  some of the biophysical properties were  of the pretend neurons that might be  disrupted and that could give rise to  these changes in the neural signaling  properties of burkini neurons that that  output signal of the king neurons  and so again we did um recordings in  acute slices in this case we're doing  whole cell patch recordings  using a voltage clamp method and we can  then it use voltage steps to  create current changes in the cell and  identify and examine specific  membrane properties in particular we  looked at the membrane resistance the  membrane time constant and the membrane  capacitance we found abnormal  differences in the double mutant mice  across all three of these metrics and in  particular we did find almost a half  half the membrane capacitance in the  double mutant mice  and this means that the  double mutant mouse purkinje neurons are  likely about have half the membrane of  that of the of the wild type and this is  consistent with a shrinking of the  pretense neurons um that would be  consistent with the shrinking of the  molecular layer with the ricking neuron  dendrites and a predominant amount of  their membrane is composed  we then also looked at synaptic inputs  into the purkinje neurons to look to see  whether there was maybe dysregulation of  other cells within the  in  the cerebellar cortex and consistent  with the fact that the  granule cell layer didn't show any um  loss in their width we didn't find any  uh  defects in the synaptic inputs of uh the  granule cells to the purkinje neurons  here we looked at the mini excitatory  postsynaptic currents these are the  currents that are just generated  spontaneously from the granule cell  parallel fiber synaptic endings onto the  kidney neurons  and there are two traces here that you  can see at the top one in purple and one  in orange  we didn't find any differences in the  amplitude the average amplitude of the  currents and this suggests that  the  granule cells are not showing any  perturbations we did however find a  difference in the frequency and this  would suggest that there are actually  twice as many granule cell inputs onto  the purkinje neurons and this is an  interesting finding that we hope to  further investigate in the future  we also looked at the paired pulse  facilitation which is a normal response  of parallel fibers so this is a  short-term  form of plasticity if you stimulate the  parallel fibers twice in short  succession in this case just 50  milliseconds  the second pulse the second current  response is usually larger than the  first and we found no difference in  the paired pulse facilitation in the  granule cell  parallel fiber inputs onto the prequel  neurons which further supports the idea  that the granular cell synapoxide that  pre-synapse in the granule cells was  normal  we did however find some abnormal  uh inputs from the climbing fiber which  is the other major input into the  prekinder neurons and these differences  suggest defects in the intrinsic calcium  currents within the pretense neurons  the in the case of climbing fibers  instead of paired pulse  facilitation  one normally finds paired pulse  depression that is that the second  current response to a double  uh stimulation of the axons the climbing  fibers results in a smaller current  response rather than a larger one  and you can see that in the double  mutant mice the  size of that second response is even  smaller than that of the  the wild type we also we did however  find that the overall amplitude of what  is really an all or none synaptic input  from the climbing fibers was not  different between the  wild type and double mutants and this  suggests that the climbing fiber synap  presynaptic side is normal however we  did find significant differences in both  the half width the with  the half width and the decay rate of the  current  in the  climbing fibers and this suggests that  there's actually an intrinsic um  challenge  in the calcium currents within the  in the purkinje neurons  as these that the currents that are  recorded are predominantly mediated  by calcium flux  these mice  showed other characteristic  uh  symptoms of at they showed reduced  weight and survivability and they also  showed a high uh pride election and  predisposition for cancer  here i'm just showing the weight of the  animals over age and you can see that in  the male the  double mutants are about half the weight  by p 200 and in females they're about a  third they're about two-thirds the  weight of the  control mice  any mouse that lacks atm at all whether  whether or not there is any aptx showed  reduced survivability that is that they  had predisposition in this case for  cancer  we in these pie charts here you can see  that  the bottom three on the right there  which are all of the atm deficient mice  with no with all some or no aptx all had  a high preponderance in the bluish gray  for developing  a  thymoma  cancer in the thymus and that in large  part these  um thymomas were responsible for  the  50 survival rate of the mice that you  can see over on the left and the  probability of survival in mice that  lack the atm protein  these mice also show immune defects  which i'm not going to talk about or  tell you show you any data on today but  they these mice  for the first time  show the broad spectrum of  characteristic symptoms of at and thus  provide us for the first time a mouse  model to not only examine  the underlying mechanisms that link atm  deficiency and aptx deficiency  to cerebellar dysfunction and atrophy  but also as a potential tool  to test therapeutic approaches  and  understand whether any potential  therapies might have any therapeutic  efficacy and as of such right now  a major challenge within  the development of at therapies has in  part been that there's been no animal  model with which to really test them  before it gets um to the clinic  so in conclusion um as i stated here  just now um our novel mouse model of at  um  mouse model of at deficient mice and  that lack both atm and aptx  develop a progressive and severe ataxia  the ataxia is associated with cerebellar  atrophy resulting from progressive  shrinkage of the dendritic length of the  purkinje neurons and that the attacks is  also associated with abnormal neural  activity and membrane properties of the  praking neurons  the synaptic inputs to prekinder neurons  appear relatively normal but its  response properties suggest defects in  the intrinsic calcium currents and or  homeostasis within pretense neurons  in the future we're hoping to expand the  analysis of the contributors to the  cerebellar atrophy to see whether  other cells not just the granule cells  and the parking neurons may  not just the pretending neurons are  affected it looks like the granule cells  which is the most predominant cell type  in the cerebellar cortex a purifying  we hope we will plan to look at the  cerebellar nuclei as well as  non-neuronal types of cells glia et  cetera like the bergming glia  we plan to further define the abnormal  forking neuron properties and their  implications on disrupting cerebellar  output and those effects on control of  motor coordination because of course the  underlying ataxia here is unlikely  generated by  significant loss of purkinje neurons as  it doesn't look like they  are dying off but that they are  dysfunctional and that this dysfunction  is changing the neural activity and  output of the cerebellum which is likely  contributing to the  the motor defects and finally we look to  elucidate the link between why dna  repair pathway proteins like atm and  aptx when deficient in mice uh  when deficient in mice and humans causes  cerebellar defects and atrophy and  resulting in ataxia  i'd like to thank the a  number of people who have contributed to  this project  in particular those  past and present who have worked in my  lab this includes harvey may jose martin  kim callum genie and angelina  and several of my  collaborators  uh most notably tim madrenas and  michelan  and i'd like to thank my funders  including the nints the lundquist  institute and the ctsi at ucla thank you  for taking the time to listen to my talk  and  i hope to hear from you soon if you have  any questions
 

